  "query_preview": "..\n\nMeteorological Overview:\n\nA southwesterly flow aloft will more or less persist across the \nNorthern Rockies through the weekend into early next week. This flow \nis initially more cyclonic in nature, which will foster plenty of \nshowers and thunderstorms through Friday evening across the region. \nPersistent surface winds from the east and northeast will continue \nto usher in cooler temperatures. Precipitable water values will tick \na touch higher today, resulting in increased precipitation \nefficiency with showers and thunderstorms that develop. The most \nrobust thunderstorms that develop over the next few days will be \ncapable of producing at least briefly heavy rainfall. Should one of \nthese stronger storms move over urban areas or burn scars, some \nlocalized flash flooding cannot be ruled out. Lightning and gusty \nwinds will also be a concern with stronger thunderstorms. \n\nHeading into the weekend the nature of the southwesterly flow aloft \nbecomes a bit more anti-cyclonic. That said, moisture working in \nfrom the southwest will combine with temperatures trending closer to \nnormal to result in scattered afternoon showers and thunderstorms. \n\nGreater uncertainty in the pattern develops toward the middle of \nnext week. At this point the main takeaway will be for scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms most days with temperatures near average. \n-AM\n\n- Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nFlash flooding concerns:\n\nHREF 10km neighborhood probabilities for a quarter of an inch of \nrain over an hour is at or greater than 50% over the Pioneer \nMountains today. Given the recent intense fire activity resulting in \nhydrophobic soils and steep terrain within the fire footprint, it \nwill not take much precipitation over a short period to result in \nsome flash flooding. As such, this area was added to a Flash Flood \nWatch valid for this afternoon and evening. Another Flash Flood \nWatch may be necessary for the same area for tomorrow as well. \nOngoing Flash Flood Watch for the Horse Gulch Burn Scar remains in \neffect unchanged from the previous forecast update.\n\nRisk for strong thunderstorms:\n\nWhile inverted-V soundings will be around across SW MT the next two \ndays, these inverted-Vs are rather shallow all things considered. \nThis will help reduce the risk for a strong to severe gust. At this \ntime the main concern will continue to be lightning, aside from \nbriefly heavy rainfall. -AM",
